00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
 
flawless my dear  gossip girls set in the 19th century  with gorgeous ball gowns and  scandalous romances this is exactly the  kind of netflix content we needed to get  through the winter  i wish to be entertained if you haven't  watched bridgeton yet you'll probably  want to binge it before diving into this  video so we don't spoil any of the drama  bridgeton was a huge risk for netflix  filming on the series started in july  2019 and wrapped in february 2020  just before the pandemic hit that's  almost nine months of shooting which is  pretty long for a tv show  combine that with the incredible sets  and literally thousands of costumes and  the drama-filled 19th century romance  could have easily been a disaster  thankfully shonda rhimes was behind the  breathtaking tv series and here is how  the huge undertaking all came together  behind the scenes like gossip girl  bridgerton gets its inspiration from a  book series  it was inspired by julia quinn's  best-selling novels  the first of which is fittingly titled  the duke and i  quinn is incredibly popular with about  10 million books in print in the us  alone when approached with the idea for  the show she  jumped at the chance to give shonda  creative control of the series  she read the script as a consultant but  wasn't involved in the writing  as for shonda she knew immediately after  reading the series that she wanted to  turn the burning hot romance into her  next big television event  the thing about romance novels is they  don't get taken very seriously  but by providing a luck series with  intriguing character depth  shonda is bringing the steamy romance to  the forefront of entertainment  but you might be surprised to know that  shonda wasn't the official creator  or showrunner of the series she passed  that torch over to chris van dusen  who previously worked with her on grey's  anatomy and scandal  the bridgerton books follow the eight  alphabetically named bridgeton siblings  while the concepts stayed relatively the  same there were a few changes made to  make the series more modern  for one shonda wanted a 21st century  audience to see themselves in these  characters  so diversity was very important  particularly in casting a black  queen charlotte and though the series  and show are set in the regency era  neither the show's creators nor quinn  were very concerned with historical  accuracy  they wanted to avoid the typical  historical romance tropes  which meant giving their female  characters agency despite them having to  try and conform to 19th century society  but even if perfect historical accuracy  wasn't the main concern  transporting the audience to the 1800s  definitely  was in order to get the actors fully  immersed in the regency romance  the cast spent six weeks in a boot camp  getting familiar with things like  horseback riding  regency history etiquette and dancing  this is where the cast learned more  about their characters like how the  featherington sisters are meant to be  like the kardashians of the 19th century  while the bridgertons are more like the  kennedys you know  new money versus old money penelope is  kind of like the chloe of the family  while lady portia featherington is a  kris jenner like  momager with a six-week boot camp  followed by more than  six months of filming it's no surprise  that the cast are now  incredibly close phoebe denver who plays  daphne  thinks of jonathan bailey who plays  anthony as an older brother  and claudia jesse who plays eloise is  now best friends with nicola cochlin  who plays penelope some other  interesting secrets from the stars  are that nicola cochlin was working in a  doctor's office before she landed her  role  golda rocheval didn't originally  audition for the role of queen charlotte  but for another unspecified role that  she didn't get  and jonathan bailey was on his way to  coachella when he heard the news that he  would be  lord anthony bridgerton as for being  starstruck  the entire cast did freak out about one  cast member  though they didn't even get to film  scenes with her the true scene stealer  even though she doesn't actually appear  on camera was the one and only  julie andrews who voices lady whistle  down  now you're not the only one who's  wondering what it's like for the  bridgerton stars to film those steamy  love scenes into the bedroom  but surprisingly they're not nearly as  awkward as we thought they would be  you might have noticed that daphne's  romantic scenes with simon are almost  entirely shot from her perspective  to give the show a more feminist lens as  for phoebe  and reggie champage's on-set chemistry  phoebe said the extensive preparation  ahead of filming played a large part  they actually worked with a coordinator  to help develop a behind-the-scenes  relationship with one another that would  translate well on screen  so when it came to that scene in episode  6 where daphne and simon get hot and  heavy in the library  they weren't too nervous which is good  considering that was actually the first  scene the pair  ever filmed together all of the most  romantic scenes are a huge deal to the  film  and are completely choreographed they  take at least half a day to shoot  and while the actors were all exhausted  by the end they never  once felt uncomfortable while bridgerton  is probably not the kind of show you'd  want to watch with your parents the  stars managed not to be too embarrassed  by their families watching  i think the most horrifying thing is  when your parents aren't horrified  like you're sitting there and you're  like oh phoebe admitted she warned hers  and had to make sure to distance herself  from the character of daphne so she  wouldn't feel too  embarrassed while reggae joked that he  would rather keep it a surprise  as for the real challenging scenes  though they had nothing to do with  romance  the most difficult ones were actually  those involving horses me and people  particularly were very  happy and confident with our horses  until they said action  for those working behind the scenes  creating the costumes was  incredibly difficult the show has 7  500 outfits over eight episodes and it  took five months to make them all  ellen mirage nick was the show's costume  designer and you might know her work  from the greatest showmen  daphne alone wears 93 dresses throughout  the first season  to create unique looks for the  characters that blend modern and regency  era fashion  the designer created mood boards and  used the cut and color of clothing to  distinguish the sophisticated bridgetons  from the more superficial featheringtons  mirage neck also used a lot of  embellishments to liven up the costumes  and you'll notice a lot of layered  jewelry tulle and lace  they had a whole team in charge of  finding the perfect embellishments to  make these costumes  perfectly over the top this also meant  that each look was incredibly  complicated  especially queen charlotte's she has a  different wig and dress in  every scene and sometimes she was even  wearing two wigs to appear even more  flamboyant and exaggerated  these looks often included things like  feathers and pearls and  tons of petticoats it took half an hour  to put on the queen's costumes  and it took two people to help her get  in and out of those double corseted  outfits  and speaking of corsets the ones for the  series were designed by  an icon mr pearl he's the best corset  designer in the business and has done  looks for beyonce  madonna and the kardashians  another aspect that took as much work as  costuming was choreography  the dances were so intricate and  specifically tailored to each character  thanks to choreographer jack murphy the  dances intertwined modern movement and  time period appropriate style  the dancing was actually the part that  made phoebe denver the most nervous  since she says she was never much of a  dancer thankfully the choreographer had  their backs  and it ended up being a great bonding  experience for the entire cast  creating those massive ballroom scenes  was a huge challenge for the set design  crew as well there were around 200  people working behind the scenes to make  everything look  perfect but it was pretty hard  transforming modern mansions into 19th  century homes  one of the carpenters on set reportedly  dedicated four months  just to making time-appropriate  fireplaces and windows  so those are all the details behind  season one but  what about season two will there be one  and what will it be like  well there's no confirmation yet but  showrunner chris van dusen is definitely  up for a second season  since there are eight siblings each of  whose story is central to different  novels in quinn's series  making more seasons is definitely in the  cards it seems even more likely thanks  to a recent listing from production  weekly  which shows that season 2 is set to  start filming in uxbridge england in  march 2021  the next book in the series is titled  the vi count who loved me  and focuses on anthony's love story so  if we do get a second season of this new  drama we're all obsessed with we can  probably expect to see a lot more of  anthony  and definitely another look at daphne  and the dukes blossoming life together  would you like to see eight different  seasons each focusing on a different  bridgerton  let us know in the comments down below  don't forget to hit that like button if  you enjoyed this video and subscribe for  more  thanks for watching

